From a1a5a4f8f434a15712dd57da8307b91304076ec6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ShibbityShwab <18537102+ShibbityShwab@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 08:20:00 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 21/26] net: ethernet: hisilicon: add the Luofu LSW read-only baseline probe

Extend the D1a internal-switch skeleton to the D1b read-only baseline
probe, built from the register and qid-map facts measured on the stock
vendor boot of 2026-11-18 (INVENTORY-2026-11-18.md section 3,
LSW-SPEC.md sections 7.3 and 12.3).

The binding grows to the measured shape: 17 windows, with the 15
512 KiB MMIO register pages first in reg/reg-names order and the two
DDR regions the engine consumes (4 MiB queue, 1 MiB flow) last, so
the node unit address is the first MMIO window.  The driver looks the
resources up by name, so the window order is binding/DTS data only and
no driver change is needed.  The binding also gains the CRG syscon
phandle used for the gate and reset state, and the measured
hisilicon,port-bitmap = <0xf01 0x1ff0>.  The new
luofu-switch-regs.h holds the measured register map with an evidence
grade on every offset and value, plus the derived qid-table write
geometry documented as the paper W2 write set.

probe() is fail-closed and read-only.  It first checks the measured
powered state of the block - CRG gate word 0x20 bits 0/1 set and the
four reset lines released with the active-low polarity fixed earlier in
the series - and refuses without touching the block if that state does
not hold.  It then reads and validates 30 measured baseline words and
re-derives both qid maps from the port model, comparing all 384 words
(128 table A + 256 table B) against the measured contents.  The driver
contains no writel(), enables no clock, drives no reset, registers no
netdev and no notifier, and still exits probe with -EOPNOTSUPP; the
devicetree node stays disabled.  The qid-table write geometry is
documented but not executed.

The write campaign is not closed and the attended W2-W6 checks are
still owed: W2 exists only as a paper write set validated read-only on
one boot, W3 is the first own-kernel clock/reset write, W4 writes one
qid table and sends one frame, W5 adds the remaining populated rows and
W6 verifies learning-off/forward-all.  A second boot or unit read-only
inventory is also needed to test whether the measured baseline is
stable.  What the writes do to the hardware, the meanings of the port
ids and the layout of the other switch tables remain [U].

This is an RFC and not mergeable.  The driver has never run on hardware:
the values it carries were read from the stock vendor system by a
fail-closed read-only inventory tool, not by this driver, and it writes
no register.  No boot, no image and no flash is involved.

Host-compiled against linux-6.18.60 in the target lane (0 warnings,
0 errors under CONFIG_WERROR=y); the extended binding passes
dt_binding_check and the board DTS compiles with zero warnings.

Signed-off-by: ShibbityShwab <18537102+ShibbityShwab@users.noreply.github.com>

---
 drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/Kconfig             |   11 
 drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/luofu-switch-regs.h |  214 +++++
 drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/luofu-switch.c      |  874 ++++++++++++++++----
 .../bindings/net/hisilicon,luofu-switch.yaml       |   82 +-
 4 files changed, 962 insertions(+), 219 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/Kconfig b/drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/Kconfig
@@ -182,16 +182,17 @@
 	  If you are unsure, say N.
 
 config LUOFU_SWITCH
-	tristate "HiSilicon Luofu internal LSW switch (draft, probe refuses)"
+	tristate "HiSilicon Luofu internal LSW switch (draft, read-only probe)"
 	depends on OF
 	help
 	  Platform driver for the internal Ethernet switch of the Hi5671Y
 	  "luofu" SoC, the switch core between the GEMAC ports and the PHY side
 	  of the wired data path.  This is a draft port: it validates the
-	  register windows, the clocks, the resets and the devicetree switch port
-	  model, then refuses to program the switch until the register, queue and
-	  port-id model has been verified against the running hardware.  The
-	  board devicetree keeps the node disabled.  Say N unless you are
+	  register windows, the clocks, the resets, the devicetree switch port
+	  model and the measured register/qid-map baseline, then refuses to
+	  program the switch until the register, queue and port-id model has
+	  been verified on the running hardware.  It never writes a register.
+	  The board devicetree keeps the node disabled.  Say N unless you are
 	  working on that port.
 
 endif # NET_VENDOR_HISILICON
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/luofu-switch-regs.h b/drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/luofu-switch-regs.h
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/luofu-switch-regs.h
@@ -0,0 +1,214 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only */
+/*
+ * HiSilicon Hi5671Y "luofu" internal LAN switch - measured register map.
+ *
+ * Every offset and value in this file is either recovered from the vendor
+ * hi_lsw_pub/hi_lsw_dp/hi_lsw_pfe modules (call-site register addresses; the
+ * block reverse engineering is documented in LSW-SPEC.md) or measured on the
+ * stock vendor boot with the 2026-11-18 read-only bench inventory (600/600
+ * reads; the switch group was 476 of them, all stable in two passes).  The
+ * evidence grade is marked per item:
+ *
+ *   [V] measured on the running stock vendor system with the read-only
+ *       inventory: the vendor driver's post-boot state on this board
+ *       generation (hwver 0x250).  A value is only [V] for that one boot;
+ *       it is a baseline to validate against, not a promise about writes.
+ *   [I] inferred from the vendor disassembly: the code shape is [V], the
+ *       field/register meaning is an interpretation and has never been
+ *       confirmed by a write experiment.
+ *   [U] unknown: listed only so the model's gap is stated.  Nothing [U]
+ *       may be programmed from this file.
+ *
+ * The driver built from this header is READ-ONLY.  It maps the MMIO windows
+ * and, only after the block has been checked to be clocked and out of reset,
+ * reads the baseline registers and the two qid maps and validates them.
+ * It never writes a register, never enables a clock, never drives a reset and
+ * never registers a netdev; probe() always fails closed.
+ *
+ * The qid-map write recipes recovered from the vendor code are documented
+ * here (offsets, slot arithmetic, the 0x100 enable flag) because they are the
+ * D1b W2 write set, but no code in the driver issues them until the attended
+ * W4 experiment signs the model off (LSW-SPEC.md section 12.3).  This patch
+ * deliberately does not model or program the EQM indirect command word, the
+ * queue-table entry layout, the classifier/L2/VLAN table formats or the
+ * queue/flow DDR layout: all of those are [U] and must not be written as
+ * fact.
+ *
+ * Safety rule for the read set: the driver only reads offsets that the
+ * 2026-11-18 inventory read on the running block, plus the two qid maps it
+ * read completely.  An access outside that set is not "probably fine", it is
+ * unreviewed, and this driver does not make it.
+ */
+
+#ifndef __LUOFU_SWITCH_REGS_H
+#define __LUOFU_SWITCH_REGS_H
+
+#include <linux/bits.h>
+#include <linux/sizes.h>
+#include <linux/types.h>
+
+/*
+ * The register/DDR windows of one switch.  The binding/DTS order puts the 15
+ * register windows first and the two DDR regions last (the enum order below
+ * is internal: resources are looked up by name).  The register pages are five
+ * lsw_dp and ten lsw_pfe windows, all [V] from the vendor devicetree and
+ * confirmed on the live post-overlay node ("lsw_dp" five + "lsw_pfe" ten
+ * windows).  The DDR regions are validated by base/size and never ioremapped.
+ */
+enum luofu_switch_window {
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_QUEUE,		/* DDR: engine queue memory */
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_FLOW,		/* DDR: engine flow memory */
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_DP,		/* lsw_dp register page */
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_IPS,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_FAM,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_PE,		/* lsw_pfe windows below */
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_EQM,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_EPS,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_PRBS,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_PFE_GLB,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_PA,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_IFC,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_L2,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_NAPT,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_MCC,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_OPP,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_PUB,
+	LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_NUM,
+};
+
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_NUM_DDR_WINDOWS	2
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_NUM_MMIO_WINDOWS	\
+	(LUOFU_SWITCH_WIN_NUM - LUOFU_SWITCH_NUM_DDR_WINDOWS)
+
+/* Every MMIO window of the block is 512 KiB. [V] */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_WINDOW_SIZE	SZ_512K
+
+/*
+ * The two DDR windows.  The vendor base devicetree says 0x87b00000/4 MiB and
+ * 0x87f00000/1 MiB, but the board U-Boot overlay rebases them to the bottom
+ * of DDR; the live post-overlay node was read back byte-for-byte on
+ * 2026-11-18 and is [V]:
+ *
+ *   queue  0x80000000  4 MiB
+ *   flow   0x80400000  1 MiB
+ *
+ * The regions sit below the kernel's memory node (0x80500000), so Linux never
+ * maps them as RAM.  Their *layout* inside the regions is [U]; nothing in
+ * this driver touches them.
+ */
+/*
+ * CRG gate word and the two switch gate bits [V] (MISC-SOC-BLOCKS.md 5.2,
+ * measured live value 0x02f8300b on the stock boot): bit 0 = lsw_dp_clk0,
+ * bit 1 = lsw_pfe_clk0, 1 = enabled.  The clock consumer API in 6.18 has no
+ * "is this gate enabled" query (clk_is_enabled() was removed), so the driver
+ * reads this word through the CRG syscon regmap - a read-only use of the
+ * shared page - before its first register access.  It never writes it.
+ */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_CRG_GATE_WORD	0x20
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_CRG_DP_GATE	BIT(0)
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_CRG_PFE_GATE	BIT(1)
+
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_QUEUE_BASE		((phys_addr_t)0x80000000)
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_QUEUE_SIZE		SZ_4M
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_FLOW_BASE		((phys_addr_t)0x80400000)
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_FLOW_SIZE		SZ_1M
+
+/*
+ * dp window: the probe-time configuration words.  The vendor hi_dp_probe
+ * writes these after the reset pulse [V code shape, [I] field meaning]:
+ *
+ *   dp+0x1c  = (u16 probe state | 0x10000)
+ *   dp+0x64  = 1, then the "cell-size" selector 0..3
+ *   dp+0x68  = 0 (memory number)
+ *
+ * The measured post-boot baseline is [V]:
+ *   dp+0x1c = 0x00018000, dp+0x64 = 1, dp+0x68 = 0
+ */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_DP_PROBE_CFG	0x1c	/* [V] 0x00018000 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_DP_CELL_SELECT	0x64	/* [V] 1 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_DP_MEM_NUMBER	0x68	/* [V] 0 */
+
+/*
+ * eps window: queue/DFX state and the PIE channel map.  hi_pie_map_init
+ * writes the per-channel map words at eps+0xB00 and a companion word 0x80
+ * above them, for the PIE channel range; the measured live words are [V] raw
+ * values with [U] field meaning (LSW-SPEC.md section 7.4).
+ */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_EPS_QUEUE_DFX	0x1008	/* [V] 0 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_EPS_CH_MAP(ch)	(0xb00 + (ch) * 4)	/* [V] */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_EPS_CH_COMPANION(ch)	(0xb80 + (ch) * 4)	/* [V] */
+
+/*
+ * eqm window: egress queue manager.  hi_queue_init reads +0xa4/+0x18 and
+ * writes +0x18, +0x38 and +0x40 (bit31 = enable), then uses the indirect
+ * table interface at +0x48 (command/status) and +0x5c (write data) [V code
+ * shape].  The indirect command-word bit layout is [U] and deliberately not
+ * modelled.  Measured baseline [V]:
+ *
+ *   eqm+0x00 = 0x00000001   eqm+0x18 = 0x80001cc0
+ *   eqm+0x38 = 0x000007f0   eqm+0x40 = 0x80000000
+ *   eqm+0x48 = 0x41100012   (bit31 busy = 0, bit5 error = 0: idle)
+ *   eqm+0xa4 = 0x00001fc0
+ */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_EQM_TAB_CMD	0x00	/* [V] 1 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_EQM_CELL_CFG	0x18	/* [V] 0x80001cc0 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_EQM_CELL_CFG1	0x38	/* [V] 0x000007f0 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_EQM_ENABLE		0x40	/* [V] 0x80000000 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_EQM_IND_CMD	0x48	/* [V] 0x41100012 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_EQM_IND_DATA	0x5c	/* [V] 0x00000bb8 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_EQM_TAB_CFG	0xa4	/* [V] 0x00001fc0 */
+
+/* eqm indirect command/status idle bits. [V] */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_EQM_IND_BUSY	BIT(31)
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_EQM_IND_ERROR	BIT(5)
+
+/* fam window: queue-count/cell comparison in the probe. [V] 0x00002000 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_FAM_QUEUE_CFG	0x504
+
+/*
+ * ips window: the probe init writes +0x10 |= 0x10000 and +0x08 = 42
+ * [V code shape]; measured baseline [V]:
+ *
+ *   ips+0x00 = 0x00000000   ips+0x08 = 0x0000002a
+ *   ips+0x10 = 0x00790011   (bit16 set)
+ */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_IPS_PROBE_CFG0	0x00	/* [V] 0 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_IPS_PROBE_CFG1	0x08	/* [V] 0x2a = 42 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_IPS_PROBE_CFG2	0x10	/* [V] 0x00790011 */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_IPS_INIT_BIT	BIT(16)	/* [V] set */
+
+/*
+ * opp window: the two qid maps.  qid_map_set(a port, slot) writes
+ * (0x100 | qid) with these two index shapes [V code shape]:
+ *
+ *   port 0..15:  table A at opp+0x42800, word index port*8 + slot
+ *   port 40..47: table B at opp+0x43800, byte offset
+ *                ((port - 40)*8 + slot) * 16, and the *same* word is
+ *                written to the four consecutive u32 words of the 16-byte
+ *                entry (a 128-bit table row)
+ *   port >47:    a third, different shape (word index slot/2 + port*4 - 64
+ *                of table A); its write shape is recovered only partially
+ *                and is [U], so ports above 47 are not modelled here
+ *   port 16..39: no entry; the vendor function prints "invalid" and returns
+ *
+ * The measured table contents (both fully read, identical in two passes)
+ * are [V]: table A has ports 4..12 populated with qids 1..72, table B has
+ * ports 40..43 populated with qids 73..104, every non-zero word carries the
+ * 0x100 enable flag, and all other rows are zero.  That is exactly the
+ * model "one contiguous qid space from 1, 8 qids per port, allocated to the
+ * populated port rows" - which the driver's read-only check re-derives and
+ * compares (D1b W2: validate the write model without writing).
+ */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_OPP_QID_TABLE_A	0x42800
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_OPP_QID_TABLE_B	0x43800
+
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_QID_SLOTS		8
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_QID_TABLE_A_PORTS	16
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_QID_TABLE_B_PORTS	8
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_QID_TABLE_B_FIRST	40
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_QID_ENABLE		BIT(8)	/* 0x100 flag */
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_QID_MASK		GENMASK(7, 0)
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_QID_FIRST		1
+#define LUOFU_SWITCH_QID_LAST		104
+
+#endif /* __LUOFU_SWITCH_REGS_H */
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/luofu-switch.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/luofu-switch.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/luofu-switch.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/hisilicon/luofu-switch.c
@@ -1,95 +1,95 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
 /*
- * HiSilicon Hi5671Y "luofu" internal LAN switch - D1a resource/binding draft.
+ * HiSilicon Hi5671Y "luofu" internal LAN switch - D1b read-only baseline probe.
  *
- * STATUS: HOST-ONLY PROBE SKELETON.  NO REGISTER IS READ OR WRITTEN, NO CLOCK
- * IS ENABLED, NO RESET IS DRIVEN, NO INTERRUPT IS REQUESTED AND NO NETWORK
- * PORT IS REGISTERED.  probe() validates the documented resources and the
- * switch port model, then fails closed with -EOPNOTSUPP.
+ * STATUS: HOST-ONLY, READ-ONLY.  The driver maps the switch windows and, only
+ * after checking the CRG gate word (read-only, through the CRG syscon) and the
+ * reset-line states that the block is in the measured powered state, reads the
+ * measured baseline registers and the
+ * two qid maps and validates them against the 2026-11-18 read-only bench
+ * inventory.  It NEVER writes a register, never enables a clock, never drives
+ * a reset, never registers a netdev and never installs a switchdev notifier.
+ * probe() always fails closed with -EOPNOTSUPP; the board devicetree keeps the
+ * node disabled, so a normal boot never reaches this point.
  *
- * Why probe must not program the switch yet
- * -----------------------------------------
- * The switch (the vendor's hi_lsw_pub/hi_lsw_dp/hi_lsw_pfe module family) has
- * no source in any tree and no datasheet. Its register map, queue maps and
- * classifier table formats were recovered offline from the vendor modules,
- * but none of that model has been measured on this board: several items are
- * explicitly unknown (the devicetree port_bitmap decoding, the mapping from
- * the MAC port numbers to the switch's internal port ids, the EQM indirect
- * command word, the high port classes of the qid maps, the VLAN/L2 table
- * formats). An access to an unknown part of this block can cut the
- * CPU/console path, and the board has no validated in-kernel restart path, so
- * a probe that programmed the switch from inferred semantics would trade the
- * only working configuration for no evidence. The D1a shape implemented here
- * is therefore: bind and validate, map the windows without touching the
- * block, and refuse.
+ * Why the block must stay read-only in this patch
+ * ----------------------------------------------
+ * The switch has no source and no datasheet.  Its register map, port/queue
+ * model and table formats were recovered offline from the vendor modules, and
+ * the first attended read-only inventory (2026-11-18) measured the vendor
+ * driver's post-boot state and both qid maps.  That is the D1b "W1" baseline.
+ * What is still missing is the part that matters before any write: the meaning
+ * of most fields, the EQM indirect command word, the queue-table entry layout,
+ * the classifier/L2/VLAN table formats and the layout of the queue/flow DDR
+ * regions are all unknown.  A write built from inferred semantics could cut
+ * the CPU/console path on a board with no validated in-kernel restart path, so
+ * the D1b write set has to be reviewed and then validated by the attended
+ * W3-W6 experiments first (LSW-SPEC.md section 12.3).  This patch is the
+ * read-only half of that work: it turns the measured baseline into a checkable
+ * model and it fails closed on anything that does not match it.
  *
- * What the switch is
- * ------------------
- * The wired path is GEMAC -> internal switch -> PHY -> RJ45; the SoC owns the
- * queue/egress decision and the classification/forwarding tables, while the
- * host side of the data path is the separate PIE packet engine. There is no
- * DSA-style CPU-port tag anywhere in the recovered model and every wired port
- * already has its own netdev (created by the GEMAC driver), so the switch is
- * modelled as a switchdev switch core with per-port netdevs, not as a DSA
- * switch. The vendor's own switchdev handlers are compiled stubs in the
- * shipped build, so the offload layer is ours to write, not to copy.
- *
- * What this draft validates
- * -------------------------
- * - 15 MMIO windows of 512 KiB each: the five queue-manager windows
- *   ("dp", "ips", "fam", "eqm", "eps") and the ten
- *   classification/forwarding windows ("prbs", "pfe_glb", "pa", "ifc", "l2",
- *   "napt", "mcc", "opp", "pub", "pe"). The vendor devicetree describes the
- *   same block as two nodes (a queue-manager node and a parser node); they
- *   are one switch, and this driver owns both window sets so the binding is
- *   complete before any part of the block is ever programmed.
- * - two CRG gate clocks (lsw_dp_clk, lsw_pfe_clk) and the four CRG reset
- *   lines of the block (dp_logic, dp_srst, pfe_logic, pfe_srst). They are
- *   acquired and named, not acted on: enabling the gates or pulsing the
- *   resets is a bring-up operation, not a probe-time fact.
- * - the switch port model: one child per port under "ethernet-ports", each
- *   carrying a port index (reg) and an "ethernet" phandle to the node that
- *   provides that port's netdev. The index is a devicetree ordering index,
- *   not the recovered hardware port id: the mapping between the MAC port
- *   numbers and the switch's internal port ids is one of the unknowns above.
- * - the SoC variant through the OF match data (v250; the vendor devicetree
- *   carries hwver = <0x250> on both switch nodes and the recovered probes
- *   branch on that generation).
+ * What the probe validates
+ * ------------------------
+ * - the 17 devicetree switch windows: 15 MMIO pages of 512 KiB that are
+ *   validated and mapped (mapping alone does not touch the block) and the two
+ *   DDR regions the engine consumes, whose measured live base/size after the
+ *   board loader's devicetree fixup are 0x80000000/4 MiB (queue) and
+ *   0x80400000/1 MiB (flow).  The DDR regions are validated and then left
+ *   alone: they are not register pages and this driver never ioremaps them.
+ * - the SoC variant: match data carries the vendor devicetree's hwver 0x250
+ *   ("v250") and the measured DDR bases/sizes; an unknown generation fails
+ *   closed.  The vendor hwver is a devicetree property, not a register: no
+ *   hardware ID register was found in the read-only inventory, so this driver
+ *   does not invent one.
+ * - the two CRG gate clocks and four CRG reset lines: acquired by name, never
+ *   enabled or driven.  Before the first register read the probe checks that
+ *   both gate bits read back enabled (gate word 0x20 bits 0/1, read-only
+ *   through the CRG syscon: the 6.18 clock consumer API has no "is enabled"
+ *   query) and that all four reset lines read back deasserted.  The CRG reset
+ *   class is active-low (patch 014), so a released line reads back as status 0;
+ *   if the state is not the measured one the probe refuses WITHOUT touching the
+ *   block.  This is the checked form of the D1b ordering rule "clock gates
+ *   first, then deassert the resets" - the write sequence itself stays a
+ *   reviewed bench step.
+ * - the switch port model: the "ethernet-ports" children carry an ordering
+ *   index and an "ethernet" phandle, and the probe records the owning platform
+ *   device of each port when that device is enabled.  The per-port netdev
+ *   belongs to that device (the GEMAC driver), not to the switch driver; a
+ *   switchdev notifier cannot be registered until a real port netdev exists,
+ *   so no notifier is installed.  This is the switchdev-over-per-port-netdevs
+ *   model, not DSA.
+ * - the CRG "port_bitmap" devicetree cells: the vendor model is <0xf01 0x1ff0>
+ *   as two cells and the live node was measured unchanged.  Its semantics
+ *   (which bit means which port/queue) are still unknown, so the driver only
+ *   validates that the board declares the measured value and does not use it
+ *   to enable anything.
+ * - the measured register baseline: 30 stable words covering the dp/eps/eqm/
+ *   fam/ips probe-time configuration and the PIE channel map.  The measured
+ *   values and their evidence grades are in luofu-switch-regs.h.
+ * - the two qid maps: the probe read all 128 table-A words and 256 table-B
+ *   words on the stock boot.  The decode says "one contiguous qid space from
+ *   1, 8 qids per port, allocated to the populated port rows".  The driver
+ *   re-derives that model and compares it word for word with the hardware, so
+ *   the D1b W2 write model is validated without writing anything.
  *
  * What it deliberately does not do
  * --------------------------------
- * - no register access at all: there is no readl()/writel() in this file,
- *   no table access, no doorbell;
- * - no clock enable and no reset assert/deassert;
